<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces. Reading: Respond to questions based on class readings. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze information about healing plants in Panama (e.g., "The text describes the most common healing plants of Panama."). Can respond to questions about the types and benefits of plants in class discussions (e.g., "What is mint for?" "It is good for the stomach."). Can apply basic syllabification rules to decode and accurately pronounce two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words, enhancing phonemic awareness (e.g., "treatment," "therapeutic").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various healing plants (aloe vera, mint leaves, lemongrass, ginger) and asks students to recall their names and uses from the previous lesson. The teacher asks, "What are these plants for?" or "Have you ever used them?" to activate prior knowledge related to natural remedies and their purpose.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "aloe vera," "treatment," "therapeutic," and "lemongrass." The teacher models stating purpose: "Aloe vera *is used to* *treat* burns." and giving advice: "You *should use* mint leaves for stomachaches." The teacher also models how to identify syllables in compound words like "lemongrass."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is ginger *used to* heal infections or headaches?" (Expected answer: "Infections.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *should you* use for a small burn?" (Expected answer: "Aloe vera.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5–7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short editorial or informational article titled "Panama's Natural Pharmacy: Healing Herbs" (A2.2 level). The text describes various plants (aloe vera, mint leaves, soursop, lemongrass) and their uses, explicitly integrating "used to + infinitive," "should/shouldn't," and "present simple" for facts, along with target vocabulary (plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare, useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic, for, with, traditionally, safely).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can read and understand the main ideas and specific details in simple informational texts about natural remedies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly and answer, "What is the main topic of this article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article again to answer true or false questions (e.g., "Aloe vera is *used to* treat colds. T/F," "Soursop *is good for* infections. T/F").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match the name of the plant with its specific use or benefit as mentioned in the text (e.g., "Mint leaves" -> "relieve headaches").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10–12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice reading for specific information, applying grammatical structures (infinitive of purpose, should/shouldn't) and target vocabulary to understand and answer questions about natural remedies. They also practice phonemic awareness through syllabification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-fill Reading:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences extracted from the article, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., "aloe vera," "treatment," "infections," "boil") or grammatical structures ("is used to," "shouldn't") missing. They must read the original text to fill in the blanks correctly. Example: "Sáblia (_____) is ____ for burns."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Transformation (Advice & Purpose):</b> In pairs, students read sentences from the text and rewrite them, focusing on grammar. For example, they might transform a factual statement like "Mint leaves relieve headaches" into "Mint leaves *are used to* relieve headaches" or rephrase "Don't ignore small cuts" to "You *shouldn't* ignore small cuts."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Syllable Decoding:</b> The teacher writes a list of two-syllable, three-syllable, and compound words from the text (e.g., "treatment," "therapeutic," "application," "lemongrass," "natural") on the board. Students work individually to divide these words into syllables (e.g., "treat-ment," "ther-a-peu-tic") and then practice pronouncing them with correct stress, guided by the teacher to enhance phonemic awareness.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15–18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives 2-3 "Patient Remedy Cards." Each card describes a common ailment (e.g., "A small burn on the hand," "A bad stomachache," "A mild cold") related to the text. The students must read each card, discuss which healing plant from the article (aloe vera, mint leaves, lemongrass, ginger, soursop) is the best remedy, and collaboratively write down their recommendation. They *must* use "You *should* use..." for advice and "It *is used to*..." to state the plant's purpose. The focus is on analyzing the text to provide appropriate, relevant advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group problem-solving and written recommendation based on reading comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This activity directly applies the information read about "Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns" and other healing plants in the Panamanian context, enabling students to give practical advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and review of written recommendations. The teacher circulates, listens to discussions, and checks for accurate reading comprehension and correct use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents one of their recommended remedies and explains *why* it's suitable, reading their recommendation aloud. For example, "For a small burn, you *should use* aloe vera. It *is used to* soothe the skin."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies the healing plant for the ailment based on the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately uses "You should/shouldn't" for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately uses "It is used to" for stating purpose.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., aloe vera, burn, soothe, treat, mint leaves, stomachache, therapeutic).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of words practiced for syllabification.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper before leaving:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about healing plants today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I now understand how to use _____ (plant) for _____ (problem)."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident am I in reading and understanding texts about natural remedies?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the lesson helped me the most in understanding how to use healing plants?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully analyze the information about healing plants and respond to questions based on the reading?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was the phonemic awareness practice on syllabification effective for decoding 'treatment' and 'therapeutic'? What adjustments could make it more engaging?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did students apply the grammar (used to, should/shouldn't) in their written recommendations? Do I need to provide more scaffolded practice next time?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 3), we will transition from reading to actively *speaking* and interacting about these healing plants. We will practice discussing remedies and giving advice to each other, using the knowledge we gained from today's reading."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>